<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Imperial Abbey of Kornelimünster</h1><p class="st-meta">814 CE – 1802 CE (approx.) · capital Kornelimünster</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Carolingian abbey near Aachen, founded by Louis the Pious for the reformer Benedict of Aniane, which ruled a tiny imperial territory until 1802.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 814 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> founded a monastery at Inda, near his palace at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-aachen">Aachen</a>, for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedict-of-aniane">Benedict of Aniane</a>, the leading reformer of Frankish monasticism. In 817 a council held at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> imposed the Benedictine Rule on all monasteries of the empire, with Inda as a model house.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The abbey received relics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-cornelius">Pope Cornelius</a> in 875, and the place became known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kornelimunster">Kornelimünster</a>. Its abbots were princes of the Empire ruling a small territory, the Münsterländchen, of a few villages. Relics presented to the faithful included textiles believed to be from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passion-of-jesus">Passion of Christ</a>, shown every seven years in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-aachen">Aachen</a> pilgrimage.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The French annexed the area in 1794 and dissolved the abbey in 1802. The territory passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1815. The former abbey buildings later housed a collection of modern art, and the church continues as a parish church.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/imperial-abbey-of-kornelimunster.jpg" alt="Imperial Abbey of Kornelimünster" width="320" height="177"><figcaption>Image: Hdamm · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aachen-Kornelim%C3%BCnster_Propsteikirche_St._Kornelius.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>814 CE – 1802 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kornelimünster</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Benedict of Aniane</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ripuarian German, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kornelimünster Abbey</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1308&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-kornelimunster" aria-label="Imperial Abbey of Kornelimünster on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4821 417 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Imperial Abbey of Kornelimünster lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5141 673L5229 694M5141 673L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5141 673h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5141 673h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1308&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-kornelimunster">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

